<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.13.3: Finding Domain and Range Using a Graph</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01096</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.13.3: Finding Domain and Range Using a Graph</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b943d03e-b169-4373-ac2c-61f1fc49d1ce</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>A tennis ball was dropped from a certain height. It bounced several times, rolled along for a short period, and then stopped. Function \(H\) gives its height over time.</para>
      <para>Here is a partial graph of \(H\). Height is measured in feet. Time is measured in seconds.</para>
      <para>Use the graph to help you answer the questions.</para>
      <para>Be prepared to explain what each value or set of values means in this situation.</para>
      <para>
          <media alt="Nonlinear function. Time (seconds) and height (feet).">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_06_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Find \(H(0)\). What point is this on the graph? What does it represent?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(H(0)=6\). It tells us the ball was dropped from a height of 6 feet at \(t=0\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Solve \(H(x)=0\).</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(x=0.54\), \(x=1.48\), and \(x=2.25\) are three possible solutions, but there are others. Each one represents the time when the ball hit the ground.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Describe the domain of the function.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The domain includes all values between 0 and the time when the ball stopped moving, which, based on the graph, is about 3.8.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Describe the range of the function.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The range includes all values between 0 and 6 feet, the height from which the ball was dropped.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End --></section>
      <section>
      <title>Video: Interpreting Function Notation</title>
      <para>Watch the following video to learn more about function notation.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/20f903eb50ca52bd9b9bfa4a7db8be6af298e6be"/>
      </media></section>
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      
      <section>
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
          <section>
            <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
      
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>In function \(H\), the input was time in seconds, and the output was height in feet.</para>
          <para>Think about some other quantities that could be inputs or outputs in this situation.</para>
          <para>Describe a function whose domain includes only integers. Be sure to specify the units.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The maximum height, in feet, that the ball reaches after \(n\) bounces as a function of the number of bounces.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      
        <exercise class="RFM1">
          <problem>
            <para>Sketch a graph of this function. Write down your answer then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your answer.  </para>
        <para>(<media alt="A blank graph. Origin O. "><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_06_02.svg"/></media>)</para>
  
            <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your sketch and answer.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_06_03.svg"/>
                </media>
              
              <emphasis id="docs-internal-guid-663b6ac4-7fff-f1cb-6e91-14df360308a7"/>
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--End interaction-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>Describe a function whose range includes only integers. Be sure to specify the units.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The number of times the ball has hit the ground as a function of time, in seconds, since the ball was dropped.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      
        <exercise class="RFM1">
          <problem>
            <para>Sketch a graph of this function. Write down your answer then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your answer. 
        (<media alt="A blank graph. Origin O. "><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_06_04.svg"/></media>)</para>
            <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your sketch and answer.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="A blank graph. Origin O. ">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_06_05.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check">
      <para>The graph below shows the number of customers, \(C\), in a restaurant \(x\) hours after 8 a.m.</para>
      <para>What does \(C(14)\) equal, and what does it mean?</para>
      <para>
          <media alt="A GRAPHED FUNCTION, WHERE NUMBER OF CUSTOMERS IS A FUNCTION OF THE NUMBER OF HOURS AFTER 8 AM. THE GRAPH INCREASES, DECREASES, INCREASES, AND THEN DECREASES AGAIN.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_07_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
    </note>
    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      
    <section>
      <title>Domain and Range from Graphs</title>
      <para>The graph below shows the distance Carl drives from home, \(D(t)\), when \(t\) is time in seconds.</para>
      <para>
          <media alt="Graph on coordinate grid, origin O.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_08_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>What is the domain of the graph?</item>
      </list>
      <para>The domain is \(x\)-values that are at least 0 and a maximum of 400.</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>What is the range of the graph?</item>
      </list>
      <para>The range is the \(y\)-values that are at least 0 and a maximum of 285.</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>What are the values of \(x\) where \(D(x)=0\), and what do they mean?</item>
      </list>
      <para>\(D(0)=0\) and \(D(400)=0\), and this is when Carl left home and then arrived home, so the distance was 0.</para>
      <note class="try">
  <title>Try It: Domain and Range from Graphs</title>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>What are the values for the domain and range of the graph below?</para>
            <para>
                      <media alt="A graph. ">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_08_02.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Here is how to find the domain and range of the graph:</para>
            <para>The domain includes the \(x\)-values or inputs of the graph. The domain is from at least 0 to at most 10.</para>
            <para>The range includes the \(y\)-values or outputs of the graph. The range is from at least 1 to at most 3.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
    </section>
    
